## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-time propagation in three-valued logic, simulates realistic policy engine scenarios, and validates backtracking capabilities with multi-step constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic
- Cross-temporal constraint satisfaction
- Backtracking with propagation and pruning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple two-period CSP checker with Kleene semantics
function kleeneNot(x) { if(x==='T') return 'F'; if(x==='F') return 'T'; return 'U'; }
function kleeneOr(a,b) { if(a==='T'||b==='T') return 'T'; if(a==='U'||b==='U') return 'U'; return 'F'; }
function kleeneAnd(a,b) { if(a==='F'||b==='F') return 'F'; if(a==='U'||b==='U') return 'U'; return 'T'; }
function kleeneImplies(a,b) { return kleeneOr(kleeneNot(a), b); }

// Check constraints across both periods
function validateConstraints(t0, t1) {
  const withinT0 = [
    kleeneImplies(kleeneOr(t0.C1, t0.C2), t0.C3),
    kleeneOr(kleeneNot(t0.C5), t0.C4),
    kleeneImplies(kleeneAnd(t0.C2, kleeneNot(t0.C3)), kleeneOr(t0.C1, t0.C5))
  ];
  const withinT1 = [
    kleeneImplies(kleeneOr(t1.C1, t1.C2), t1.C3),
    kleeneOr(kleeneNot(t1.C5), t1.C4),
    kleeneImplies(kleeneAnd(t1.C2, kleeneNot(t1.C3)), kleeneOr(t1.C1, t1.C5))
  ];
  const cross = kleeneImplies(kleeneOr(t0.C1, t1.C4), t0.C2);

  return [...withinT0, ...withinT1, cross].every(v => v !== 'F');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply Kleene three-valued logic to a realistic policy constraint problem and to design a simple backtracking propagation strategy for constraint satisfaction.

## Key Concepts
- **Kleene three-valued semantics**: Truth values T (true), F (false), and U (unknown). Operations: ¬U = U; A ∧ B = F if either is F, else U if either is U, else T; A ∨ B = T if either is T, else U if either is U, else F.
- **Implication**: A → B is equivalent to ¬A ∨ B.
- **Constraint propagation**: Systematically assign values and propagate logical consequences, backtracking when contradictions (F) arise.

## Code Example
```javascript
const values = ['T','F','U'];
function not(a) { return a === 'T' ? 'F' : a === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U'; }
function lor(a, b) { return a === 'T' || b === 'T' ? 'T' : a === 'U' || b === 'U' ? 'U' : 'F'; }
function land(a, b) { return a === 'F' || b === 'F' ? 'F' : a === 'U' || b === 'U' ? 'U' : 'T'; }
function imply(a, b) { return lor(not(a), b); }

function evaluate(vars) {
  const c1 = imply(land(vars.M1, vars.M2), vars.M3);
  const c2 = imply(lor(vars.M2, vars.M4), vars.M5);
  const c3 = imply(not(vars.M5), lor(vars.M1, vars.M4));
  return { c1, c2, c3 };
}

const example = { M1: 'U', M2: 'T', M3: 'T', M4: 'F', M5: 'U' };
console.log(evaluate(example)); // { c1: 'T', c2: 'U', c3: 'U' }
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a small CSP with Kleene semantics, reason about propagation, and craft a concrete counterfactual assignment with U, plus depth-limited reasoning.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic: T>U>F; ¬ maps T↔F, U↔U.
- Implication as ¬X ∨ Y; F only when antecedent is T and consequent F.
- Depth-limited backtracking with constraint propagation to avoid exploring futile branches.

## Code Example
```javascript
function kleeneNot(v){ return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U';}
function kleeneOr(a,b){ return a==='T'||b==='T'?'T': a==='U'||b==='U'?'U':'F';}
function kleeneImp(a,b){ // a -> b
  if(a==='T' && b==='F') return 'F';
  if(a==='T' && b==='U') return 'U';
  if(a==='F') return 'T';
  if(a==='U' && b==='F') return 'U';
  return (b==='T') ? 'T' : 'U';
}
```

