## 4-Valued Backtracking Propagation

1. **Initialization**: Start with variables unassigned
2. **Propagation**: Apply logical operators using 4-valued truth tables
3. **Pruning**: Eliminate partial assignments that force any rule to f
4. **Backtracking**: When pruned, backtrack to try alternative values
5. **Solution**: Return first assignment with all rules ≠ f

The assignment A=f, B=t, C=t, D=n satisfies all constraints while including non-t values as required.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about small logical constraints and translate them into a checkable search, including vacuous implications and cardinality.

## Key Concepts

- Logical implications and vacuous truth
- Cardinality constraints (minimum true)
- Backtracking with pruning
- Generalizing to N variables

## Code Example

```javascript
function countTrue(arr){ return arr.filter(x=>x).length; }
function implies(p,q){ return !p || q; }
function sat(assignment){
  const [A,B,C] = assignment;
  if(!implies(A,B)) return false;
  if(!implies(B,C)) return false;
  if(countTrue(assignment) < 2) return false;
  return true;
}
```

